## ingest tick vs an in-flight cost read (concurrent) - review L-26
  each iteration schedules a poll tick 1 ms out, twice: once with an empty loop
  underneath it, once with a cost-summary read issued in the same turn. The lead is a quarter
  of the median read above (capped at 20 ms) so the deadline falls INSIDE the read at any scale;
  both arms use it, so it cannot bias the comparison. Lateness is
  when the tick actually started minus when it was due - the wait a libuv timer pays
  while a synchronous read holds the stack. The poll is not the only such timer: the
  SSE heartbeat is one too, and it pays the same lateness on the same loop.
                                                min     median        max
  tick lateness, nothing in flight          -1.0 ms     0.3 ms     0.3 ms   n=5
  tick lateness, cost read in flight        -0.2 ms    -0.2 ms     0.0 ms   n=5
  tick duration, nothing in flight           2.6 ms     2.8 ms     5.6 ms   n=5
  tick duration, cost read in flight         2.8 ms     3.5 ms     4.2 ms   n=5
  the in-flight read itself (wall)           0.7 ms     0.8 ms     0.9 ms   n=5
  tick outcomes: baseline [unchanged], contended [unchanged] - the arms are only comparable while these match.
  NOT RESOLVED at this scale: the gap between the arms
  (-0.6 ms between the fastest contended tick and the slowest baseline one) does not clear
  this run's own jitter (baseline spread 1.3 ms, floor 1.0 ms). Do NOT quote a delta
  from this run - re-run at a larger --sessions, where the read is longer.

